of\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"renownProfile":{"knowsBook":true,"publicFame":35,"criticalRenown":78,"controversy":18,"llmAffinity":88,"affinityResidual":12.19,"confidence":{"publicFame":0.96,"criticalRenown":0.94,"controversy":0.88,"llmAffinity":0.94},"tags":{"craft":"craft-of-explanation","evidence":"archival-research","stance":"revisionism"},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":1},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The anonymous publication of Vestiges helped initiate modern public controversies about evolution.","confidence":0.68},"academicOrientation":{"score":48,"confidence":0.8},"profileConfidence":0.82,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"fbc7f99190464a00444e2e4c97747061cac88f871ffd74971f974cf7c49408ce","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"summary":"\"Fiction or philosophy, profound knowledge or shocking heresy? When Vestiges of the Natural History of Creation was published anonymously in 1844, it sparked one of the greatest sensations of the Victorian era. As gripping as a popular novel, Vestiges combined all the current scientific theories in fields ranging from astronomy and geology to psychology and economics. The book was banned, it was damned, it was hailed as the gospel for a new age. This is where our own public controversies about evolution began.\". \"In a pioneering cultural history, James A. Secord uses the story of Vestiges to create a panoramic portrait of life in the early industrial era from the perspective of its readers. We join apprentices in a factory town as they debate the consequences of an evolutionary ancestry. We listen as Prince Albert reads aloud to Queen Victoria from a book that preachers denounced as blasphemy vomited from the mouth of Satan. And we watch as Charles Darwin turns its pages in the flea-ridden British Museum library, fearful for the fate of his own unpublished theory of evolution. Using secret letters, Secord reveals how Vestiges was written and how the anonymity of its author was maintained for forty years.","displaySummary":"When Vestiges of the Natural History of Creation was published anonymously in 1844, it sparked in fields ranging from astronomy and geology to psychology and economics. The book was banned, it was damned, it was hailed as the gospel for a new age. This is where our own public controversies about evolution began.\". \"In a pioneering cultural history, James A. Secord uses the story of Vestiges to create a panoramic portrait of life in the early industrial era from the perspective of its readers. We join apprentices in a factory town as they debate the consequences of an evolutionary ancestry. We listen as Prince Albert reads aloud to Queen Victoria from a book that preachers denounced as blasphemy vomited from the mouth of Satan. And we watch as Charles Darwin turns its pages in the flea-ridden British Museum library, fearful for the fate of his own unpublished theory of evolution. Using secret letters, Secord reveals how Vestiges was written and how the anonymity of its author was maintained for forty years.","primarySubject":"Biography","primaryTopic":"Mental Health & Psychology","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Biography","confidence":"medium","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Biography","score":6,"evidenceCount":1},{"subject":"Science","score":6,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Chambers, robert, 1802-1871","mappedSubject":"Biography","score":6,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-open-library-summary-victorian-sensation-the-extraordinary-publication-reception-and-secret-authorship-of-vestiges-of","id":"subject-evidence-book-victorian-sensation-the-extraordinary-publication-reception-and-secret-authorship-of-vestiges-of-1"},{"source":"open_library","scheme":"open_library_subject","rawLabel":"Biological Evolution","mappedSubje